{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789296075,"nonce":"lbz/m/EccD6grwzCmlKVaUABwZo=","title":"Python conformance `dict` category fails once in a while right after a build","message":"Seen twice on 2026-09-13, both times in the first `cargo test -p\nzyntax_python` after a rebuild that regenerated the snapshot (a\ncompiler or typed_ast change re-runs the frontend's build script), only\nthe `dict` category, passing on every rerun without rebuilding. Not\nreproduced by: 80 sequential and 24 parallel runs of the two dict\ncases, 25 rounds under 12 busy-loop processes, malloc scribbling over\nevery category, forced tier promotion at warm thresholds 1 to 20, and\ntwo rebuilds with a touched compiler source followed by the full suite.\nThe failure text was not captured either time.\n\nWhat the harness did that could turn a transient into a failure: it\npiped stderr and never read it (a child writing a pipe's worth would\nstall until the 60 s bound), read stdout only after exit (same for\nstdout), and reported a child killed before running (spawn refused\nunder load, or the loader refusing a just-linked binary) as a case\nfailure. All three are fixed: stderr is discarded, stdout is drained as\nit is written, and a run that died with no output is retried up to\ntwice.\n\nIf it recurs, the regression message now carries the exit status; a\nstatus of -2 with empty output is the loader, -3 a timeout, anything\nelse a real difference worth a snapshot or cache investigation\n(HirId relocation base, TypeId reservation order, tier promotion timing\nwere the candidates ruled out so far).","files":null}]}